h1 Robo-journalism script editor
p This tool allows for live editing of data-driven semi-automated journalism (AKA "robo-journalism") articles using a #[a(href="https://pugjs.org/api/getting-started.html" target="_blank") Pug template file] and a CSV data source.

p This editor is built on the ONS #[a(href="https://github.com/ONSdigital/robo-utils" target="_blank") robo-utils] JavaScript package, which includes a number of useful Natural Language Generation (NLG) tools and functions to make it easier to generate human-readable articles.    

h2 Quick start
p If you want to skip straight to the action, there are currently two demo Pug files (and associated CSVs) that you can open and edit to get a feel for how the editor works.

h3 1. Simple article demo
p A relatively straightforward example. The output of this demo is used in the #[a(href="https://github.com/ONSdigital/robo-embed" target="_blank") robo-embed] Svelte Kit template. #[button(onclick="embedDemo()" class="btn-inline") Open this template]

h3 2. Scrolly article demo
p A more complex demo, suitable for a localised scrollytelling article. The output of this demo is used in the #[a(href="https://github.com/ONSdigital/robo-scrolly" target="_blank") robo-scrolly] Svelte Kit template. #[button(onclick="scrollyDemo()" class="btn-inline") Open this template]

h2 Starting from scratch

h3 1. Load a data file
p Click #[b Load] next to #[b CSV] in the toolbar. The file should have one row per area, with an #[b areacd] column for the area's code and an #[b areanm] column for its name, plus any columns of data. Add a #[b parentcd] column if you want to refer to each area's parent, eg. its region.
p Then choose an area from the drop-down list to preview the output for it. The buttons next to the list show the data, and let you choose which rows are included.

h3 2. Write your template
p Write your Pug template in the editor on the left, and the output on the right updates as you type. To work on an existing template, click #[b Load] next to #[b PUG], or paste it into the editor.
p For a cheat sheet of how to write templates, with examples of the Pug language and the robo-utils functions and links to their full documentation, #[button(onclick="showHelp()" class="btn-inline") open the help] or click the #[b i] button in the top-right corner.

h2 Saving your work
p <i>Note: The editor saves your template and data in your browser as you work, so you shouldn't lose your progress if the window gets closed accidentally.</i>
p You can download your Pug template by clicking #[b Save] next to #[b PUG]. If you prefer, you can also just copy-paste the text from the editor on the left into an external file.
p You can also save the output for all of the places/rows in your data file in a JSON, MD or RTF format. These outputs come in the form of a ZIP file with a file inside for each place/row. For convenience, these outputs also contain the Pug and CSV files.